00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.410
So first, let me start with
the motivation behind Kubeflow

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:27.910
Pipelines.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.670
Why did we start this
project in the first place?

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.000
We started this project
because building and deploying

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.250
real-world machine
learning applications

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.820
is hard and costly because
of the lack of tooling

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.660
for the end-to-end development
and deployment lifecycle,

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.490
or the end-to-end process.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.670
Let me elaborate on
that a little bit.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.310
Often, when we talk about
building a machine learning

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.540
application, we're talking
about developing the core model.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.280
And the model
development happens

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.250
with libraries like TensorFlow
and Python, Scikit-learn,

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.060
XGBoost, and so forth.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.292
So often, we talk about
developing the model

00:02:04.292 --> 00:02:05.500
and then deploying the model.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.470
And, yes, absolutely, that
is at the heart of developing

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.930
a machine learning application.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.380
But when you are
building, deploying,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.550
and running a machine learning
application in production,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.012
that entails many other stages.

00:02:21.012 --> 00:02:22.720
A production machine
learning application

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.130
is often running continuously.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:27.040
It's running 24/7 by 365.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.380
Data is coming in
on a regular basis,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.810
either streaming in or
coming in as batches

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.090
on periodic intervals.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.270
Models have to be constantly
updated and improved

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:38.750
upon and deployed.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.200
And of course, before you
deploy any newly-trained model,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.730
you have to make sure the new
models are safe for deployment.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.610
For the data that
is streaming in,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.522
you have to do data
pre-processing and data

00:02:49.522 --> 00:02:51.480
validation and make sure
that there are no data

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.770
quality issues.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.410
Then, after that, you do
featured engineering followed

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.870
by model training with
hyperparameter tuning,

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.780
followed by model validation
and so on and so forth.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:05.100
So what is needed
is simple tooling

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.290
to orchestrate and manage these
multi-phase machine learning

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.670
workflows, and the ability
to run them reliably

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.510
and repeatedly.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.960
And that is the problem
that Kubeflow Pipelines

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.840
aims to solve.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.000
So actually, before
we move on, I

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.003
should call out
that this diagram.

00:03:25.003 --> 00:03:26.420
It's not a new
diagram we created.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.300
It's actually from a
fairly seminal Google paper

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.610
that was authored a few
years back that talks

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.370
about the hidden debt in
building machine learning

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:35.330
applications.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.820
So actually, I would like to
have a quick show of hands

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.570
to see if folks have
actually seen this diagram

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.440
or something similar before.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.460
So please raise your
hands if you've seen it.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:46.092
All right, wow.

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:47.550
That's actually
quite a few of you.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.520
So possibly, I am preaching
to the choir here,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.660
but that's awesome.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.770
All right, so before we get
into the specifics of Kubeflow

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.220
Pipelines, I want to provide
a little bit more context.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:04.310
Kubeflow Pipelines is part
of the open source Kubeflow

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.570
project.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.390
Open source Kubeflow is
a seamlessly integrated

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:11.375
collection of tools and
services for the full production

00:04:11.375 --> 00:04:12.500
machine learning lifecycle.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.820
Everything from distributed,
scalable model training

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.570
to deploying models for
online serving at scale,

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.360
to notebooks with
Jupyter hub, and then

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.280
workflow orchestration
and metadata

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.350
and workflow management
with Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.350
and so on and so forth.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.400
So Kubeflow Pipelines is a
part of this larger open source

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.400
project called Kubeflow.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.070
Kubeflow services are built
to run on top of Kubernetes.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.410
And we chose Kubernetes
as the underlying platform

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.330
because, at this
point, Kubernetes

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.740
is almost a de facto for
anything that entails

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.320
cluster compute at scale.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.450
So Kubernetes provides
the scalability,

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.600
and it also enables
hybrid deployments

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.230
because Kubeflow flow can run
on any Kubernetes cluster.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.330
So that cluster can be
running on Google Cloud

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:59.850
or it can be running on-prem,
or multiple other clouds.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.450
And of course, with
the Anthos announcement

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:03.950
made two days back,
you can actually

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.070
run Google on top
of Anthos and help

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:11.450
Anthos manage these deployments
in multiple environments.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.480
And of course, on
Google Cloud, Kubeflow

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.020
is easy to deploy with Google
Kubernetes Engine or Anthos.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:21.260
And in many cases, for
applications authored

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.500
with the Kubeflow
SDKs, you can run them

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.890
on fully-managed services
in the Cloud AI platform.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.040
So I wanted to provide
that bit of context

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.530
before we dove into the
specifics of Kubeflow

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.090
Pipelines.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.120
Now, the capabilities
provided by Kubeflow Pipelines

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.150
can largely be put
under three buckets--

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.060
machine learning
workflow orchestration,

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.930
sharing reusability
and easy composability,

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:50.515
and rapid, reliable
experimentation.

00:05:50.515 --> 00:05:52.640
So let's start with the
first one, machine learning

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.870
workflow orchestration.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.570
To make things more
concrete, let's look

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.810
at a screenshot of
a workflow that was

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:03.685
run with Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:06:03.685 --> 00:06:06.060
So for any workflow that you
run with Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.530
you get this rich
visual depiction

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.060
of the topology
of the workflows.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:13.400
So you can see that and
understand what was run.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.000
Of course, this is just
an illustrative machine

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.797
learning workflow.

00:06:17.797 --> 00:06:20.380
Of course, when you build your
own machine learning workflows,

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.410
you can have all sorts
of different topologies.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:24.910
For a given step
of the workflow,

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:27.960
you can use your own tools
and libraries of choice.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.840
A given step of the workflow can
be a single-node instance that

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.520
can run with different types
of hardware, GPUs and TPUs,

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:35.590
or it can also be--

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.210
a given step can
be something that

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:38.530
runs in a distributed fashion.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.200
So long story short, this is
just an illustrative workflow.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.783
And of course, you
can build all sorts

00:06:42.783 --> 00:06:46.030
of interesting topologies and
applications and architectures.

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:48.795
Now, let's look at
this specific example.

00:06:48.795 --> 00:06:50.170
In this particular
example-- just

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.790
to make things more concrete--
this particular example,

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:55.360
it starts with a data
pre-processing and data

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.600
validation step followed by
a feature engineering step.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.180
And after that,
we're forking out

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:05.230
and training three different
kinds of models in parallel.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:08.110
And each of these
model training nodes

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:11.150
encapsulates hyperparameter
tuning within it.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.180
Then, after that, we
take the best models

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:18.790
trained in each of these forks
and run them on a test data set

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:20.950
and analyze them and
compare them and then pick

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.430
the best model.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:26.680
And if that model happens to
meet our performance threshold,

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:29.620
we can deploy it to an
online serving endpoint.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.380
So that is an
example of the type

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:36.580
of workflows you can build and
deploy with Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.470
For each step of the
workflow, you actually

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:41.680
have access to all the
relevant metadata and metrics

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.220
at your fingertips.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.690
So, as an example, let's
take the training step.

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:49.000
If it produced an
auto c-curve, you

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.050
can visualize it right there.

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.410
Or if it produced the
rich metadata that

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:55.600
can be visualized
through TensorBoard,

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.698
TensorBoard is just
one click away.

00:07:57.698 --> 00:07:59.740
So long story short, all
the relevant information

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.920
that you need is available
right there at your fingertips.

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:04.690
For each step of
the workflow, you

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:08.020
have access to the configuration
parameters that were passed in,

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.430
and you can also see what
were the inputs to that step

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:12.370
and what were the
outputs produced.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:15.910
So for any module trained
with Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:17.590
you will not have
to wonder, or you

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:21.280
won't have the problem of
not being able to answer

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.380
how that model was produced.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.630
Because for any model trained
with Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:28.420
you can go back to the
corresponding execution run

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:31.568
and you have all the relevant
information at your fingertips.

00:08:31.568 --> 00:08:33.610
And this is particularly
important for production

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.169
machine learning workflows.

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:38.409
So now, let's take a
peek under the hood

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:42.669
and let's try to understand what
exactly constitutes a Kubeflow

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:44.410
Pipeline.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.540
So Kubeflow Pipeline
consists of multiple steps,

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.090
and each step of the
Kubeflow Pipeline

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:51.883
is a containerized task.

00:08:51.883 --> 00:08:54.300
We're talking about production
machine learning workflows,

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.240
and containers are the
industry standard solution

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.330
for portability,
for repeatability,

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:01.930
and encapsulation
of dependencies.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:03.555
So we don't want to
reinvent the wheel.

00:09:03.555 --> 00:09:05.898
That's why we built a solution
that leverages containers

00:09:05.898 --> 00:09:06.690
at the heart of it.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:10.110
And of course, because this
gets deployed on Kubernetes,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.900
of course we were going
to choose containers.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.420
Now, as I mentioned previously,
each step of the workflow

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:16.858
can be very
heterogeneous, can run

00:09:16.858 --> 00:09:18.150
on different types of hardware.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.970
It can be a simple Python
script or something that

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:22.320
runs in a distributed fashion.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.410
It can be an Apache Spark job,
or it could be a distributed

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.390
model training job.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.340
Steps of the workflow
can also call out

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.440
to other Google services.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.330
So those are the
steps of the workflow.

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:37.140
Now, the other key
aspect of the workflow

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.080
is definition of the
sequence, or the topology

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:40.890
of the workflow.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:43.260
The sequence in which
the steps should run

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:47.340
and how the data flows
between these steps,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:48.900
how to connect
the outputs of one

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:52.320
step with the inputs
of downstream steps.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:55.170
And then, the third aspect
of defining a pipeline

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.210
is defining the set
of input parameters

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.340
that the end user
has to provide.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.770
So the containerized
steps, or each step

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.400
packaged as a container, the
definition of the workflow

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.590
topology, and the specification
of the input parameters

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.890
together constitute
a Kubeflow Pipeline.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:14.690
So now, let's see how you
author a Kubeflow Pipeline.

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:17.070
When we spoke to the target
audience-- data scientists,

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.070
machine learning engineers,
and machine learning

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:21.170
practitioners-- time
and again, everyone

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.690
said Python is their
preferred lingua franca.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.760
So we said, OK, let's
provide an SDK that

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:28.177
is fairly intuitive and
native to Python coders.

00:10:28.177 --> 00:10:29.510
So that's what we have provided.

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:31.610
You get a fairly
intuitive Python SDK

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:35.030
that you use to specify the
topology of your workflow.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:38.180
And specifying
the topology, it's

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.263
basically as simple as calling
Python functions that represent

00:10:41.263 --> 00:10:42.680
the various steps
of your workflow

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:45.967
and connecting them
with inputs and outputs.

00:10:45.967 --> 00:10:48.050
I won't go into too many
details of the Python SDK

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:49.910
and, actually, in the
next section of the talk,

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.285
Willem, as he's doing his
demo, will provide more details

00:10:52.285 --> 00:10:53.570
about the SDK.

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:56.310
And of course, the SDK is a
broad topic in and of itself.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.510
So I'd request you to look
at the Kubeflow Pipelines

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:02.280
documentation to learn
more about the SDK.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.780
Now, at this point, you may be
wondering, but wait a minute,

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:07.340
I said each step of the workflow
is a containerized task.

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.382
And here I am talking
about defining the workflow

00:11:09.382 --> 00:11:11.900
with a Python SDK and calling
the steps Python functions.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:13.360
So, there's some
disconnect here.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.010
Valid observation.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.540
In its simplest form--

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.060
because we're
orchestrating containers--

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.040
as long as the containers
follow a protocol

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:23.890
on how they accept inputs
and how they produce outputs,

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:28.060
any containerized task can be
converted into a workflow step.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:31.790
And so that provides
flexibility.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:35.020
In fact, we have a
customer that has

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:38.530
taken Fortran libraries
that do numerical analysis,

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:40.360
put them in a
container, and called

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:43.690
that particular container as
a task in a Kubeflow workflow.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.720
So this provides a lot of
flexibility, the ability

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.670
to take a containerized
task and convert it

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:49.930
into a separate workflow.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:53.410
And the way you do that is
you define a Python function

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:57.370
wrapper for that container
by invoking this SDK function

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:58.630
called the container ops.

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:00.232
You call the
container op and you

00:12:00.232 --> 00:12:01.690
convert the Python
wrapper function

00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:03.065
that you can now
use while you're

00:12:03.065 --> 00:12:05.940
defining the workflow topology.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.960
But we realized that our target
audience-- data scientists

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:10.640
and machine learning
practitioners--

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.320
they don't come in
and build docker

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:14.120
containers on a daily basis.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:15.620
Rather, they're not
directly dealing

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.010
with docker on a daily basis.

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.520
And in many cases,
for the most part--

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:21.470
and we'll continue to
improve upon this--

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:24.290
for the most part,
the dockerization

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:27.000
becomes an
implementation detail.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:31.070
So if you're writing a step
of the workflow with Python,

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.740
we provide simple utilities
and function decorators

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:37.280
to take your Python code and
package it up automatically

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:38.808
as a containerized
task that you can

00:12:38.808 --> 00:12:40.100
call as a step of the workflow.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.400
So a key aspect here is
while, under the hood,

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:46.010
each task is a container,
for the most part,

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:48.320
machine learning practitioners
and data scientists

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:51.050
will deal with their
Python code and we

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:52.970
take care of the mechanics
of dockerization.

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:55.440
We simplify the
dockerization for you.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.680
And of course, if you're
running it on Google Cloud,

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:00.380
you are going to
call and leverage

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.990
Google Cloud's amazing
fully-managed solutions

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:05.150
for data analytics
and machine learning

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:06.750
model training and so forth.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.710
And for these services, we give
you out-of-the-box connectors.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:12.140
So again, you don't have
to focus on the mechanics

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:13.430
of calling out these services.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:14.600
We simplify that for you.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.230
You focus on your code and
you use these out-of-the-box

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:17.932
connectors.

00:13:17.932 --> 00:13:20.390
And of course, we will continue
to improve upon this stuff.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:23.220
All right.

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:26.190
So now, let's look at the
second key value proposition

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:27.815
of Kubeflow Pipelines,
which is sharing

00:13:27.815 --> 00:13:29.460
reusability and composability.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:32.850
And the key premise here is most
machine learning applications

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:35.260
today, they're built as
bespoke applications.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:37.770
But as machine learning
becomes more mainstream,

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:40.510
it becomes a key aspect of--

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:41.370
it goes mainstream.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:43.890
It's a key aspect embedded
into multiple applications.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:46.740
You need a faster way to build
machine learning applications,

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:49.260
and the best way to
do that is the ability

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:53.577
to reuse the great work
that others have done.

00:13:53.577 --> 00:13:55.410
You don't want to always
start from scratch.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:57.660
If someone else has
built an amazing workflow

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:00.030
or an amazing
component that can be

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.610
used as a part of the workflow,
we want to enable that.

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:06.023
We want you to be able to find
it easily, quickly, reuse it,

00:14:06.023 --> 00:14:06.940
and you're good to go.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:10.870
And, of course, you'll customize
it for your specific needs.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:14.010
So with Kubeflow Pipelines, once
you've defined the workflow,

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:16.580
you can package it
up as a zip file.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:19.380
And that zip file can
be shared with others,

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.785
and they can run it on
their own Kubeflow cluster.

00:14:21.785 --> 00:14:23.562
And because each task
is containerized,

00:14:23.562 --> 00:14:25.270
you're not worried
about the portability.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:28.140
So package up your Kubeflow
Pipeline as a zip file

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:29.190
and give it to--

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.550
let's just say you develop the
workflow on your development

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:34.830
cluster and you've
tested it it looks good.

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:37.020
And you want to hand it
off to the production

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.960
team that will deploy it
on the production cluster.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:41.790
Great, just package
it up as a zip file

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:45.000
and hand it over to them.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.947
And often, what you want to
do is, if you're working,

00:14:47.947 --> 00:14:49.530
say, in a large
organization or you're

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:51.072
part of a broader
community, you want

00:14:51.072 --> 00:14:53.790
to build an amazing application
and share it with others

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:56.290
so that they can leverage it
and run it and build upon it.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:15:00.600
So, if I were to find a Kubeflow
application packaged as a zip

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.580
file from someone
else, I can load it up

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:05.840
in my Kubeflow cluster,
and I will get this UI--

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:09.510
a UI form that is automatically
created based on the inputs

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.370
accepted by that pipeline.

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:13.267
I'll get that UI form
and, without having

00:15:13.267 --> 00:15:14.850
to write too much
code, I can actually

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.750
fill in the parameters
of the UI form

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:19.360
and I can try out a Kubeflow
application very easily.

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:22.230
So that's a key aspect of
being able to reuse and quickly

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.460
try out the work that
someone else has done.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.870
And this reusability is not
just for full end-to-end machine

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:30.780
learning workflows or
a complete pipeline.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.720
We've actually made it such
that an individual step

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:38.420
of a workflow can be packaged
up as a reusable component.

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:40.920
So let's just say you're doing
some very interesting feature

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.620
engineering, or you've come
up with a very interesting way

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.570
to do model analysis
that you want everyone

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:47.130
within your
organization to follow.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:47.760
Well, great.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.760
Author that as a usable
Kubeflow Pipeline component

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:52.890
and they can use it easily
within their own custom

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:54.190
workflows.

00:15:54.190 --> 00:15:56.190
And because we're talking
about reuse and share,

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:58.410
I have to give a
shout-out to the AI Hub.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.020
It's a product we launched
publicly yesterday,

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:05.010
and the AI Hub is your one-stop
shop for sharing and discovery

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.080
of relevant machine
learning assets.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:10.050
It has multiple types of machine
learning assets, from models

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:11.460
to notebooks and so forth.

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:15.390
And of course, a noteworthy
category of assets in AI Hub

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.517
will be Kubeflow Pipelines and
Kubeflow Pipeline components.

00:16:18.517 --> 00:16:20.100
And just to make
things more concrete,

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:22.410
I want to give you an
example of how easy it

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.680
is to find a reusable
component in the AI hub

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.370
and use it as a part of your own
custom machine learning, custom

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.420
Kubeflow Pipeline.

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:33.100
It literally is a
couple lines of code.

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:36.210
So, you first start with this
lowered component statement.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:39.480
You go to the AI Hub, you find
your Kubeflow Pipeline usable

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:40.400
component.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.180
There'll be a Copy URL button.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:44.670
You hit the Copy URL
button, you get that URL.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:47.250
You then use it in this
load component statement

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.760
and you get the Python function
representation of that step.

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.452
And now you can use that step
as a part of your workflow

00:16:53.452 --> 00:16:54.910
when you're defining
your workflow.

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:56.640
So long story short,
this whole aspect

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.580
of reusing the great work
that others have done,

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:01.470
or if you've done
great work, being

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.050
able to share that with others
is a key aspect of Kubeflow

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:07.859
Pipelines, in conjunction
with AI Hub aims to enable.

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:09.900
And that's a key
aspect of scaling out

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:12.770
production machine learning.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:15.859
Now, let's talk about the
third key value proposition

00:17:15.859 --> 00:17:17.240
of Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:19.190
And to motivate
this, I actually want

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:25.130
to call out a quote from one
of the winners of the latest

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:28.630
gaggle 1 million
prize competition.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:31.430
So one of the members of the
team that won the competition

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:34.940
said that for every
idea that worked,

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:38.000
they tried 100
ideas that failed.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:40.660
And that is the nature of
production machine learning.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:41.660
It's an applied science.

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:43.445
You have to try
many different ideas

00:17:43.445 --> 00:17:45.320
to find the ones that
would work on your data

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:46.860
for your application.

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:49.370
So, long story short,
we want to make it easy

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:52.130
for you to rapidly
iterate on your ideas,

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:55.520
but do so in a reliable
and manageable way.

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:58.250
So, for any workflow
or pipeline that

00:17:58.250 --> 00:18:02.420
is run on the Kubeflow
Pipelines environment--

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:04.730
on a Kubeflow cluster--

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:08.780
you get the full historical view
of all the prior runs, as well

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:10.310
as your current runs.

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:12.560
Not only that, you actually
have filtering and search

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:15.900
functionality to
quickly find a past run.

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:18.380
And once you find a past run
that you're interested in,

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:21.710
of course, you'll have all the
relevant metrics and metadata,

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:25.340
config parameters, and so
forth at your fingertips.

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:28.580
And then, you can
hit the Clone button

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.170
and create a clone
of that workflow

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:31.628
and just run that
clone if you just

00:18:31.628 --> 00:18:34.970
want to repeat a prior
experimental run,

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.640
or you can clone it.

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:41.150
You'll get the UI form that
shows all the input parameters,

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:43.070
and you can tweak
the input parameters

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:46.340
and launch a slightly different
variant of this workflow

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:48.650
to try a different idea.

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:51.530
So we've made it very easy
to hit the Clone button,

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:54.710
tweak the variant with different
configuration parameters,

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:55.710
and run it.

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.260
And in the future,
we'll actually

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:00.560
extend that to enable
rapid iteration

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:03.980
with the code pertaining to a
specific step of the workflow

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:04.563
as well.

00:19:04.563 --> 00:19:06.230
So right now, you can
do rapid iteration

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:08.930
with configuration parameter
changes and, in the future,

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:11.850
we want to enable that
for code changes as well.

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.000
So, at this point, you've
done rapid iteration.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:16.970
You've launched many different
variants of your workflow

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:20.280
to see what works best for
your data, for your use case.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.220
So let's just say
you have 20, 30,

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:24.803
or more different executions.

00:19:24.803 --> 00:19:26.970
Of course, most of those
are actually going to fail.

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:30.623
Not every execution is going to
result in the desired metrics.

00:19:30.623 --> 00:19:32.540
So now you have this
long list of experiments.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:36.680
And in the List view of
these experimental runs,

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:38.662
you get to see the
most important metrics

00:19:38.662 --> 00:19:39.870
right there in the list view.

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:42.080
So if you've got, say,
20, 30 different execution

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.510
runs, you can quickly
hone in on the ones that

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:47.687
performed well or produced
interesting results

00:19:47.687 --> 00:19:49.520
and ignore the ones
that didn't perform well

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.390
or didn't have
interesting results.

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:54.560
Select the ones that
are interesting.

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:56.900
Select a subset of experiments
that are interesting.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:20:00.020
And then hit the
Compare Runs button

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:04.743
to get a side-by-side comparison
of these execution runs.

00:20:04.743 --> 00:20:06.410
And you can do a
side-by-side comparison

00:20:06.410 --> 00:20:08.762
of the different config
parameters and metrics

00:20:08.762 --> 00:20:09.720
and so on and so forth.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:12.500
So you can quickly
hone in on what

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:14.690
was different about
these executional runs.

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:18.410
You can quickly hone in on
the technique or the idea that

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:20.730
led to interesting results.

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:23.570
So that's how we enable rapid,
reliable experimentation.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:26.060
It's reliable
because the workflow

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:31.872
itself is backed by containers,
which can be run repeatedly.

00:20:31.872 --> 00:20:33.830
All your relevant config
parameters and metrics

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:37.020
are right there at
your fingertips.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:37.520
All right.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:40.690
So those were the key
features of Kubeflow Pipelines

00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:42.868
that I wanted to
provide as an overview.

00:20:42.868 --> 00:20:45.160
I do want to mention that we
have quite a few customers

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:46.202
using Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:20:46.202 --> 00:20:47.185
We have Descartes Labs.

00:20:47.185 --> 00:20:49.060
And it's possible some
of you actually caught

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:51.820
their presentation, I
think, two days back.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:55.060
They use it for interesting
work on satellite images.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:57.220
We also have Carousell,
an e-commerce company,

00:20:57.220 --> 00:21:01.900
using it for ranking of
images and scoring images

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:04.940
and tagging them with relevant
metadata and so forth.

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:07.240
We have Baker Hughes
using Kubeflow pipelines

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.550
for workflows on
industrial data--

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:12.620
metrics collected from oil
and gas rigs and so forth.

00:21:12.620 --> 00:21:14.900
And lastly, because Kubeflow
Pipelines is open source,

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:16.450
we actually have
a vast ecosystem

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:18.250
of partners contributing to it.

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:19.990
We have Intel that has--

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:23.560
as an example, Intel has
contributed components

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:25.990
to Kubeflow Pipelines,
particularly a component that

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:30.210
makes it easy to take models
and run them on Intel's OpenVINO

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:31.960
runtime.

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:34.884
And with that, I'd like to
pass things over to Willem.

00:21:38.508 --> 00:21:39.800
WILLEM PIENAAR: Thanks, Aanand.

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:44.060
Hello, everybody.

