00:23:19.318 --> 00:23:21.735
Like I said, we're in four
different countries right now--

00:23:21.735 --> 00:23:24.840
Singapore, Vietnam, and Thailand
coming online in the last year.

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:28.620
Our ride-hailing service has
more than 2 million drivers

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:31.380
on the platform, hundreds
of thousands of which

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:33.710
are online at any
given point in time.

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:37.870
And we process more than 100
million bookings every month.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:40.170
So the scale is quite large,
and the ability for us

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:44.130
to impact the company with data
science and machine learning

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:45.515
is quite great.

00:23:45.515 --> 00:23:47.640
So I want to talk about
one of the first challenges

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:51.870
that we faced at GOJEK with
machine learning and data

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:56.070
science, and that was
finding the right driver.

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:58.080
It's a classic problem
in ride-hailing,

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:00.940
and that is dispatch
or allocation.

00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:02.880
Which driver do you
send to a customer

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:04.530
when they want to
make a booking--

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:06.600
when they're making a booking?

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:08.850
So this is an important
question because of the amount

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.267
of transactions
that are processed

00:24:10.267 --> 00:24:14.730
by a ride-hailing
company like GOJEK.

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:16.230
A system that
makes this decision

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:18.730
can have a very big impact on
the bottom line of the company

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:20.933
if you're looking at millions
of orders every month.

00:24:20.933 --> 00:24:23.100
So this was one of the first
things the data science

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:26.850
team needed to solve, is build
a system that could decide which

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:29.310
driver to send to a customer.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:32.910
And we originally started
with one North Star metric,

00:24:32.910 --> 00:24:35.470
and that metric was conversion.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:38.700
So what is the likelihood
that a successful trip will

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:40.620
be completed between
a specific pair

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:42.420
of a customer and a driver?

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:45.570
And so, when we set out
to build the system,

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:48.542
we looked at that
singular objective.

00:24:48.542 --> 00:24:50.250
And then, as a data
science platform team

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:52.440
and as a data science
team, we came together

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.050
and we built the
systems that actually

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:58.090
could deploy this model
into a serving environment.

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:01.160
So we implemented airflow for
our data pipelines and machine

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:04.753
learning pipelines, and
we implemented Kubernetes

00:25:04.753 --> 00:25:05.670
for our model serving.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:09.180
The data scientists went to work
in building the pipelines that

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:12.360
transformed and
trained the models,

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:14.970
and they deployed
it into the serving.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:19.065
And we integrated with the GOJEK
back end and it worked well.

00:25:19.065 --> 00:25:20.940
The back end would send
us a list of drivers.

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:22.482
We'd re-route those
drivers according

00:25:22.482 --> 00:25:24.570
to most likelihood
of converting,

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:28.920
and we'd choose the top
driver in most cases.

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:30.770
And there was an
uplifting conversion.

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:33.460
So we all thought it
was a job well done.

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:36.210
We made an impact
to the bottom line.

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:39.660
But it turns out that
our needs were greater

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:42.900
than that, greater than
just a single model.

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:45.942
And the thing we realized
was that machine learning

00:25:45.942 --> 00:25:47.650
should provide many
levers to a business.

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:50.460
And in this case, we had
provided only a single lever,

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:53.670
and that was an
objective of conversion.

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:57.870
But actually, in a company
that does ride-hailing,

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:59.290
it's actually a marketplace.

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:03.175
So there's customers and drivers
meeting in this marketplace.

00:26:03.175 --> 00:26:05.550
And there are many different
things you can optimize for.

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:07.500
You can optimize
for business metrics

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:12.210
like conversion, acceptance,
cancellation, costs or profit.

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:14.580
You can optimize for
the customer experience.

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:17.040
You can optimize for the
drivers' opportunities

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:19.380
and the fairness of assigning
the work because they're

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:22.800
ultimately employed, or at
least earning an income,

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:25.350
through this platform.

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:28.530
So we knew that we
needed to support

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:32.610
more types of objectives in our
system, not just conversion.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:34.260
And we needed to be
able to experiment

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:35.555
in a more granular way.

00:26:35.555 --> 00:26:36.930
So in a different
time and place,

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:39.790
we wanted to be
able to, let's say,

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:41.580
optimize for cancellation
and conversion

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:44.460
and, in another place, optimize
for the drivers' experience

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:46.540
or their opportunities.

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:51.060
So what we then decided to
do was improve our system.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:56.130
Make this driver allocation
system a multi-objective system

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:57.960
that has many different
subcomponents,

00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.250
many different models.

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:03.520
And then that would allow us
to, on a more granular level,

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:09.550
decide on a per-request basis,
what weighting of an objective

00:27:09.550 --> 00:27:11.270
to rank these drivers on.

00:27:11.270 --> 00:27:14.970
And this worked OK
for our serving layer,

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:17.970
because it's a relatively
small problem building

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:20.460
an ensemble hybrid
system like this.

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:25.680
But it turns out that taking our
existing pipelines in Airflow

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:28.020
and scaling it up
to meet the needs

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:33.510
of a generic, multi-market,
multi-service, multi-objective

00:27:33.510 --> 00:27:36.410
system was actually
very, very difficult.

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:38.160
And it was difficult
for three key reasons

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:40.010
that I'll share with you now.

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:47.438
The first reason was it was very
difficult for us to experiment.

00:27:47.438 --> 00:27:49.230
So the typical workflow
of a data scientist

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:51.210
is one of starting
in the notebook.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:52.970
And they will do
their development

00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:54.720
and then they'll get
up to a certain point

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:58.205
where they feel comfortable with
something that they've made.

00:27:58.205 --> 00:27:59.830
Maybe they're training
a model and they

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:01.860
see some good performance and
they want to productionize it.

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:03.818
They want to get into
production because often,

00:28:03.818 --> 00:28:06.265
with ride-hailing,
the only proven model

00:28:06.265 --> 00:28:10.920
is once it gets into production,
not offline in a notebook.

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:13.290
So they're always keen
to get it into production

00:28:13.290 --> 00:28:15.130
as quickly as possible.

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:19.020
And when you transfer this code
and rewrite it into Airflow,

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:21.180
one of the things that
happened in our experience

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:24.270
was that it became tightly
coupled to Airflow's

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:27.060
model of operators.

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:31.290
And so experimentation went
from the notebook to Airflow.

00:28:31.290 --> 00:28:33.330
And Airflow is great at
doing data processing,

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:34.980
and it's really
meant for that use

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:38.100
case of transforming data
and updating partitions

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:40.710
in BigQuery or a
data store, but we're

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:42.850
talking about an ML use case.

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:45.180
We struggled to
scale up to the needs

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:47.850
of our multi-objective,
multi-market, multi-region

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:48.790
system.

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:52.290
So in the ML use case, you
want to quickly iterate

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:53.850
on your pipelines.

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:56.820
You want to quickly inject
configuration or data,

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:00.390
quickly add variance,
try TensorFlow or XGBoost

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:03.630
and see the results and
evaluate the results.

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:05.670
But this was very difficult
because of the fact

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:09.510
that Airflow's based on
chronological or time-based

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:11.880
runs meant for scheduling.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:15.030
And so, variance within Airflow
was kind of tricky for us

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:16.030
to do.

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:18.930
And so, what ended up
happening is we had--

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:21.330
a lot of the workflows
were being forked,

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:23.310
and the graphs ended up
growing, and there was

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:25.450
a lot of duplication of work.

00:29:25.450 --> 00:29:29.110
So the experimentation
was very tough for us.

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:31.140
The second problem
we had was there

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:33.660
was a lot of boilerplate
code being written.

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:37.290
And this was purely due to the
fact that the orchestration

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:38.560
layer--

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:40.740
the platform itself didn't
provide this abstraction

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:41.800
to the end user.

00:29:41.800 --> 00:29:45.150
And so we knew that we needed
to solve this to allow the data

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:48.180
scientists to focus more
on, actually, the modeling

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:50.340
and what their specialty is.

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:55.690
And the third thing that
we were having a pain with

00:29:55.690 --> 00:29:58.450
was traceability
and reproducibility.

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:00.190
So with our system,
we didn't have

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:02.770
a means of injecting
dependencies, so data

00:30:02.770 --> 00:30:04.910
or configuration or
any kind of variance.

00:30:04.910 --> 00:30:06.700
So what would end up
happening is the data

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:09.910
scientists would retrieve
these dependencies as side

00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:12.100
inputs in their pipelines.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:16.300
And these side inputs were
almost always untracked,

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:20.290
and it led to us having
very little reproducibility.

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:22.913
Because if we reran that
pipeline and those side inputs

00:30:22.913 --> 00:30:24.580
had changed in the
meantime, then you'll

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:26.000
get different outputs.

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:28.690
So we knew we needed
a way to solve

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:31.900
this problem of side inputs
and untracked dependencies

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:35.260
in our pipelines.

00:30:35.260 --> 00:30:37.150
And so, we looked at a
lot of the tools that

00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:41.830
were available to us outside,
and we were already on GCP,

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:44.680
and one of the tools and
systems that we evaluated

00:30:44.680 --> 00:30:48.180
was Kubeflow Pipelines
for this use case.

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:51.460
And the way we ended up
approaching and solving

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:54.230
this problem was as follows.

00:30:54.230 --> 00:30:56.080
So for experimentation,
we introduced

00:30:56.080 --> 00:31:01.150
Kubeflow for the ML pipeline
part of our data and ML

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:02.000
pipelines.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:04.490
So we split our
pipelines into two.

00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:10.240
We still use Airflow for
our data transformations

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:12.310
and publishing to
a feature store.

00:31:12.310 --> 00:31:13.910
And this feature
store then becomes

00:31:13.910 --> 00:31:16.930
the interface between
our ML pipelines

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:18.590
and our data pipelines.

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:22.930
So now, this has the effect
of allowing your ML pipelines

00:31:22.930 --> 00:31:24.700
to be less data-focused.

00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:27.430
Even though there's occasionally
some transformations there,

00:31:27.430 --> 00:31:30.520
the pipelines are a lot smaller
and they run a lot faster.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:32.830
And with Kubeflow
Pipelines, you can spin up

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:36.040
many different pipeline
instances, runs,

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:38.890
and you can inject
parameters into those runs.

00:31:38.890 --> 00:31:40.520
And you can even do
it from a notebook.

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:42.670
So you can quickly do a
lot of experimentation

00:31:42.670 --> 00:31:45.700
rapidly and compare these
results very easily.

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:51.540
And to reduce the amount
of boilerplate code,

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:54.150
we introduced
Kubeflow components

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:56.590
to our data scientists
and our users.

00:31:56.590 --> 00:31:59.057
So these components are
pre-built by Google,

00:31:59.057 --> 00:32:00.390
and a lot of them already exist.

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:06.210
You can use to use APIs or
Dataproc or any kind of systems

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:07.920
for common operations
that you can

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:10.000
do in your Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:14.910
But you can also offer
your own custom components.

00:32:14.910 --> 00:32:18.243
And this allows us to,
as an engineering team,

00:32:18.243 --> 00:32:20.160
abstract the users away
from common operations

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:22.500
like retrieving data
from phishers, stores,

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:26.130
or publishing to our
custom in-house deployment

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:28.600
environments or
anything like that.

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:31.290
So this is how we partly
solved some of the engineering

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:34.930
challenges and
abstracted those away.

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:36.692
And then, finally,
one of the key things

00:32:36.692 --> 00:32:38.400
in Kubeflow Pipelines
that is very useful

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:41.230
is the parameterization
of the pipeline itself,

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:44.200
which was lacking in our
existing orchestration system.

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:47.430
So, by allowing
parameterization--

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:50.130
if you trace the
inputs to a pipeline

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:53.620
and if you track the actual
pipeline that executed it,

00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:55.200
and if those inputs
are immutable,

00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:56.700
then you'll
automatically get things

00:32:56.700 --> 00:32:59.550
like traceability,
reproducibility,

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:01.163
and deterministic runs.

00:33:01.163 --> 00:33:02.580
And this was one
of the key things

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:04.860
that we needed
because, if you're

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:07.920
tracking the metadata of
a pipeline and the model

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:09.760
that it produces
goes into production

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:12.090
and it performs well or
doesn't perform well,

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:14.257
you ought to be able to go
upstream again, and back,

00:33:14.257 --> 00:33:16.750
and see why that was.

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:20.790
What led to that model
performing well or poorly?

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:23.222
But let's go to a quick
demo where I can show you

00:33:23.222 --> 00:33:25.180
Kubeflow Pipelines in a
little bit more detail.

00:33:29.920 --> 00:33:33.230
So this is the Kubeflow
Pipelines user interface.

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:35.670
And if you click on
the pipeline section,

00:33:35.670 --> 00:33:38.010
it takes you to some of
the pre-built pipelines.

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:39.930
You'll see the sample
ones are the ones that

00:33:39.930 --> 00:33:44.280
are just given to us when you
deploy Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:46.980
I've built one pipeline here
and deployed it already,

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:48.660
which we'll have
a look at shortly.

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:51.510
So these pipelines are
essentially templates.

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:54.550
They're not runs or pipelines
that are executed in the past,

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:55.680
but we can run them.

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:57.270
We can start them
as far as execution

00:33:57.270 --> 00:34:00.250
runs with our parameters.

00:34:00.250 --> 00:34:02.520
So I'm going to
click into this one

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:05.410
that I've built for allocation.

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:08.340
So this is a very simple
pipeline, a very simple example

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:11.040
to just illustrate how
Kubeflow Pipelines works.

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:15.949
So what you can see here is
multiple steps in a pipeline.

00:34:15.949 --> 00:34:17.949
There are a few features
from the feature store,

00:34:17.949 --> 00:34:20.310
a training of a model, the
building of a prediction set,

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:22.590
the evaluation of that set,
and then the publishing

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:26.199
of that depending on
the evaluation criteria.

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:27.960
So if you click into
one of these tasks--

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:30.429
one of these steps--

00:34:30.429 --> 00:34:34.719
each step is a docker image
that will be run as a container.

00:34:34.719 --> 00:34:38.929
So you can actually see, at the
bottom of this page, the docker

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:41.190
image that will be run.

00:34:41.190 --> 00:34:43.380
So Kubeflow Pipelines
also tells you

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:48.989
which input parameters
will be sent to this step,

00:34:48.989 --> 00:34:51.360
and how these inputs will
map to docker container.

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:54.045
So the fact that this
is a docker image that

00:34:54.045 --> 00:34:55.420
will be run as a
docker container

00:34:55.420 --> 00:34:58.110
means that development,
debugging, testing

00:34:58.110 --> 00:34:59.020
is very easy.

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:00.690
You can do this--
all this actually

00:35:00.690 --> 00:35:02.880
can be done in a way
that is completely

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:05.537
decoupled from Kubeflow itself.

00:35:05.537 --> 00:35:07.620
And you also don't really
have any kind of lock-in

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:08.200
to Kubeflow.

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:12.400
So if you're moving from
Kubeflow to Airflow,

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:15.460
that process will be simple.

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:16.980
So, this example--
one more thing

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:18.355
that I wanted to
show you here is

00:35:18.355 --> 00:35:21.510
that you can actually see
that the inputs map from one

00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:24.120
task, one step into another.

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:29.850
And this specific graph has
been built both from a notebook

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:31.710
and published
previously, but I'll

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:33.620
go into a little bit
more detail later

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:36.880
in showing you how to
build this from a notebook.

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:38.820
So if you have a pipeline
already registered

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:41.210
with Kubeflow Pipelines, you
can create an instance of it

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:42.750
by clicking on Create Run.

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:47.200
So you can give your
pipeline run a name

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:49.590
and you can choose an
experiment, an experiment being

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:53.530
nothing more than a
grouping of comparable runs.

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:55.980
But for us-- and in terms
of the three problems

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:58.890
we had-- was rapid
experimentation being the first

00:35:58.890 --> 00:36:01.000
and the third being
the traceability.

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.120
But the key thing
that we needed was

00:36:03.120 --> 00:36:07.030
being able to inject these
parameters into our runs.

00:36:07.030 --> 00:36:09.360
So this section is
really critical for us,

00:36:09.360 --> 00:36:11.520
being able to
generalize our pipeline,

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:14.610
extract the complexities
and the things that change,

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:16.830
and create parameters from them.

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:19.180
And that allows us to more
easily scale to many markets

00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:20.910
because we can have
parameters to define

00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:23.810
service types or the region
where a specific pipeline

00:36:23.810 --> 00:36:26.680
should be targeting.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:30.100
And so these parameters
were really key for us.

00:36:30.100 --> 00:36:32.280
So instead of looking
at these parameters,

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:34.630
I can actually show
you the notebook

00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:37.580
that created this pipeline.

00:36:37.580 --> 00:36:43.150
So if you click on the side
here on the Notebooks button,

00:36:43.150 --> 00:36:45.490
it'll actually direct you to
a Jupyter instance running

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:46.782
in the same Kubernetes cluster.

00:36:46.782 --> 00:36:48.550
So when you deploy
Kubeflow, it actually

00:36:48.550 --> 00:36:52.320
deploys a Jupyter deployment
with that Kubeflow Pipelines

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:53.140
deployment.

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:56.450
And these two are able to
communicate with each other,

00:36:56.450 --> 00:36:58.700
and it's a little bit more
abstract from the end user.

00:36:58.700 --> 00:37:00.520
So if you click on
this pre-built notebook

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:03.190
that I have here for
us, I can walk you

00:37:03.190 --> 00:37:06.380
through the process of creating
and authoring this pipeline.

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:08.560
So how this works
is you initially

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:11.650
start with some steps of
housekeeping and defining

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:15.550
some global variables, one
of which that is important

00:37:15.550 --> 00:37:19.300
is defining your
Kubeflow SDK package.

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:20.913
And then, once
you have that URL,

00:37:20.913 --> 00:37:23.080
you can just install it
with PIP so the installation

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:25.000
is super easy.

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:26.867
And then you're ready to go.

00:37:26.867 --> 00:37:28.450
So the first thing
we do is we define,

00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:31.510
or we connect this
Kubeflow client, this SDK,

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:36.400
to our local Kubeflow
Pipelines deployment.

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:39.100
It'll automatically find the
Kubeflow Pipelines deployment

00:37:39.100 --> 00:37:40.300
if it's in the same cluster.

00:37:40.300 --> 00:37:41.967
If it's in a remote
cluster or if you're

00:37:41.967 --> 00:37:45.040
working on a local environment,
you need to specify a URL.

00:37:45.040 --> 00:37:46.870
Then, you specify
an experiment name.

00:37:46.870 --> 00:37:48.490
And this name, in
our case, will just

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:51.500
be allocation because that's
what we're trying to solve.

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:53.360
So this will just be a grouping.

00:37:53.360 --> 00:37:55.780
And if you actually go back
to the experiments that

00:37:55.780 --> 00:37:59.050
are available here, I've
already done some runs,

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:03.350
and there are some historic
runs already existing.

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:07.220
So the next step is
defining the pipeline.

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:10.360
This is the critical
part of actually using

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:11.500
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:38:11.500 --> 00:38:13.970
And there are basically
two steps to this.

00:38:13.970 --> 00:38:16.540
The first is defining
the operations that

00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:19.120
will be executed, and then
secondly is connecting those

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:20.720
up into a graph.

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:21.890
So what we do is--

00:38:21.890 --> 00:38:24.100
I think what Aanand was
talking about earlier

00:38:24.100 --> 00:38:27.160
is calling this
class of container

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:29.980
up and creating instances
of these operations.

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:32.770
And we parse in the
arguments that we

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:36.060
want to define as what
makes this operation unique.

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:38.500
And the two most important
parts here, which docker image

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:41.650
will be run and what will
be the arguments that we

00:38:41.650 --> 00:38:42.882
send to that docker image.

00:38:42.882 --> 00:38:45.340
And again, this makes debugging
and testing and development

00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:48.270
very easy because you can
test the specific operation--

00:38:48.270 --> 00:38:51.310
the step in your pipeline--
independently of Kubeflow

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:52.660
Pipelines.

00:38:52.660 --> 00:38:55.720
You also need to give it
a name and just tell it

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:59.980
where output data will be
available once it's completed.

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:03.580
And we wrap this whole container
operation in a function call

00:39:03.580 --> 00:39:06.670
because this makes
calling this, or creating

00:39:06.670 --> 00:39:10.370
instances of this operation
later, a little bit simpler.

00:39:10.370 --> 00:39:13.630
So in this case, we've got five
of these operations-- retrieval

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:15.610
of features, the
training of a model,

00:39:15.610 --> 00:39:18.420
creating a prediction
set, and then

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:20.620
an evaluation step
with an RSC-curve,

00:39:20.620 --> 00:39:22.970
and the publishing of the model.

00:39:22.970 --> 00:39:25.850
Now, the most important part
is defining the pipeline.

00:39:25.850 --> 00:39:29.090
So, here, it's nothing
more than a function call.

00:39:29.090 --> 00:39:31.270
So we've just called
it Allocation Pipeline.

00:39:31.270 --> 00:39:34.600
And the two important
parts here would be, first,

00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:37.270
defining the input parameters.

00:39:37.270 --> 00:39:38.770
And these arguments
to the function

00:39:38.770 --> 00:39:41.020
call actually become
the parameters

00:39:41.020 --> 00:39:42.740
that are available to you here.

00:39:42.740 --> 00:39:44.907
So if you go back to the
pipeline that we've created

00:39:44.907 --> 00:39:46.840
and you create a
run, these parameters

00:39:46.840 --> 00:39:48.850
that are available
to you actually

00:39:48.850 --> 00:39:52.220
come from the arguments
of this function call.

00:39:52.220 --> 00:39:54.130
So this is great for
us in a lot of ways

00:39:54.130 --> 00:39:56.770
because, often, we're
authoring these pipelines

00:39:56.770 --> 00:39:58.380
and the end users
are not coders.

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:00.790
They're just maybe
business users or analysts

00:40:00.790 --> 00:40:04.810
that just want to
do some analysis

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:08.930
or just explore what an
impact the model can have

00:40:08.930 --> 00:40:10.400
and just experiment a bit.

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:15.228
So by publishing and compiling
this workflow into something

00:40:15.228 --> 00:40:17.020
that can be deployed
to Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:19.297
end users can just use a
user interface and interact

00:40:19.297 --> 00:40:20.380
with the system like that.

00:40:20.380 --> 00:40:22.190
They don't need to
be able to code.

00:40:22.190 --> 00:40:23.920
So then we decorate
this function

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:26.870
call with Kubeflow Pipelines
decorator, and give it a name

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:28.710
and a description, and
then, importantly, we

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:30.130
define the steps.

00:40:30.130 --> 00:40:32.760
And here, we've got a
feature achievable step.

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:35.440
So we just call the function
we've defined already.

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:38.030
We specify the input parameters.

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:40.360
These input parameters
are the same ones

00:40:40.360 --> 00:40:42.820
that are coming into
this function call.

00:40:42.820 --> 00:40:44.890
And here's the important part.

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:46.472
The second step is
the training step,

00:40:46.472 --> 00:40:48.430
and it depends on the
first step because you're

00:40:48.430 --> 00:40:50.013
mapping the output
from the first step

00:40:50.013 --> 00:40:52.190
into this second step.

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:54.670
So you're not actually
building the graph directly,

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:57.370
but you're specifying
inputs and outputs.

00:40:57.370 --> 00:41:00.520
And once you've
completed these steps,

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:04.070
you can compile this pipeline,
and I'll do that right now.

00:41:04.070 --> 00:41:06.760
So I've compiled this pipeline
and this allocation zip file

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:10.340
will be available in
this Jupyter environment.

00:41:10.340 --> 00:41:12.380
And this can be
submitted to Kubeflow.

00:41:12.380 --> 00:41:14.260
And once you've
submitted it, it'll

00:41:14.260 --> 00:41:16.640
be available here as
part of your pipelines.

00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:20.500
But a more interesting way
that we use Kubeflow Pipelines

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:23.710
is actually starting
the runs using

00:41:23.710 --> 00:41:27.790
this allocation, or
this compiled pipeline,

00:41:27.790 --> 00:41:29.080
from the notebook.

00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:31.360
So the Kubeflow SDK--

00:41:31.360 --> 00:41:34.500
the pipeline SDK allows you
to run a pipeline directly.

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:38.690
So you can specify experiment,
give your pipeline run a name,

00:41:38.690 --> 00:41:41.080
specify the pipeline, and
then inject the parameters.

00:41:41.080 --> 00:41:43.468
And this allows for that
rapid experimentation.

00:41:43.468 --> 00:41:45.010
So in this case,
what I'm going to do

00:41:45.010 --> 00:41:46.370
is I'm going to start a run.

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:48.537
And one of the parameters
will be a list of features

00:41:48.537 --> 00:41:50.230
that we want to
send into that run.

00:41:50.230 --> 00:41:54.430
And we've also got a
target of which objective

00:41:54.430 --> 00:41:55.870
we're optimizing for.

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:58.480
So I can start a run here
and it'll create a little run

00:41:58.480 --> 00:42:02.020
link for us, and I can delete
a bunch of these features.

00:42:02.020 --> 00:42:04.035
And I can run that
again and now I've

00:42:04.035 --> 00:42:05.850
got two runs that
are being executed.

00:42:05.850 --> 00:42:07.270
I can imagine, if you want
to experiment very quickly,

00:42:07.270 --> 00:42:08.650
you can have a four-loop there.

00:42:08.650 --> 00:42:11.290
You can do a grid search
or parameter search.

00:42:11.290 --> 00:42:13.820
You can spin up 100
pipelines if you want to

00:42:13.820 --> 00:42:16.070
and compare them in
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:42:16.070 --> 00:42:17.670
So I'm just going to do this--

00:42:17.670 --> 00:42:19.420
just sort of release
those features.

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:21.170
But if we go to
Kubeflow Pipelines

00:42:21.170 --> 00:42:23.880
and we go to our
experiments, you

00:42:23.880 --> 00:42:26.640
can see that there are
now two runs running here.

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:27.960
Click on one of these.

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:30.520
Feature retrieval
has already happened.

00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:32.062
It's output a data set.

00:42:32.062 --> 00:42:34.020
And you can see the input
and output parameters

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:34.980
through this step.

00:42:34.980 --> 00:42:36.570
If you click on
Training, we can see

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:38.400
that this data
set's already been

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:40.512
loaded by the training set.

00:42:40.512 --> 00:42:41.970
This is a specific
docker container

00:42:41.970 --> 00:42:43.920
that's running inside of
this Kubernetes cluster.

00:42:43.920 --> 00:42:45.450
And you get all the
benefits of Kubernetes

00:42:45.450 --> 00:42:47.325
because it's running in
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:42:47.325 --> 00:42:50.970
So as engineers, this
allows us to build platforms

00:42:50.970 --> 00:42:53.760
on this existing tooling.

00:42:53.760 --> 00:42:56.280
Click on the logs, you'll
see that training is already

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:57.560
running.

00:42:57.560 --> 00:42:59.220
But for our use
case, I think I'll

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:02.970
show you some of the
previously executed runs.

00:43:02.970 --> 00:43:06.223
So if you click on this run,
this is a completed run.

00:43:06.223 --> 00:43:07.890
I can show you some
of the functionality

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:09.760
that Aanand was
speaking about earlier.

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:11.760
So one of the interesting
things that we can see

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:13.920
is, of course, the inputs
and outputs at each step,

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:16.562
but also the output artifacts
that are available to you.

00:43:16.562 --> 00:43:19.020
So one of the output artifacts
that we have available to us

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:22.080
here is, at the
evaluation stage, or step,

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:23.250
is an RSC-curve.

00:43:23.250 --> 00:43:26.580
So as an end user, you can
easily draw into this curve

00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:30.400
and evaluate the performance
of the specific run.

00:43:30.400 --> 00:43:32.460
But just doing this
on a single run

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:33.860
is not really that interesting.

00:43:33.860 --> 00:43:36.090
It's useful, but a
more powerful way

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:38.640
to do this is to look at
multiple of these runs

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:39.480
together.

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:42.180
So we can actually click
on multiple of these runs

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:43.643
and compare them together.

00:43:43.643 --> 00:43:45.810
And you can see that the
only thing that varies here

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:48.910
is the input parameters
of the list of features.

00:43:48.910 --> 00:43:50.582
So here, we have
more or less features

00:43:50.582 --> 00:43:51.540
for each of these runs.

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:53.530
Everything else is constant.

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:56.520
So what Kubeflow Pipelines
does is it actually shows you

00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:59.850
the output artifacts at each
stage that has the same name,

00:43:59.850 --> 00:44:01.480
but also shows you
an aggregated view.

00:44:01.480 --> 00:44:03.840
So here, you get an
aggregated view of all three

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:05.100
of these runs.

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:08.100
So you as a user can draw into
the performance of these runs

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:09.540
and compare and contrast them.

00:44:09.540 --> 00:44:11.207
So if you're spending
up many pipelines,

00:44:11.207 --> 00:44:13.400
you can quickly compare them.

00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:15.150
And even end users
that are not developers

00:44:15.150 --> 00:44:16.830
can quickly compare them.

00:44:16.830 --> 00:44:20.400
And some more of
the pipeline output

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:24.450
artifacts that I want
to show you is captured

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:27.820
in a TFX pipeline here.

00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:30.200
So this is a bit more
of a complicated run.

00:44:30.200 --> 00:44:32.097
It has more steps to it.

00:44:32.097 --> 00:44:33.930
So this is using the
New York City taxi data

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:37.560
and it's a TensorFlow
model that it's training.

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:39.360
So some of the
other artifacts that

00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:42.330
can be produced, for example,
is a confusion matrix.

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:45.450
So this matrix is
great for quickly

00:44:45.450 --> 00:44:47.250
analyzing the classifier.

00:44:47.250 --> 00:44:51.720
We've also got a table, which
is a very practical thing

00:44:51.720 --> 00:44:53.190
to expose to end users.

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:54.960
You can just quickly
get a sample of data

00:44:54.960 --> 00:44:56.270
and expose it to the end user.

00:44:56.270 --> 00:44:57.728
They don't need to
open up BigQuery

00:44:57.728 --> 00:45:00.750
or any kind of CSV file on GCS.

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:02.520
One of my favorite
output artifacts

00:45:02.520 --> 00:45:04.110
is just a static HTML.

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:06.000
As an engineer, this
unlocks a lot of doors

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:08.425
because now we can embed
any HTML and JavaScript

00:45:08.425 --> 00:45:10.800
and render anything within
Kubeflow Pipelines for our end

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:11.490
users.

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:13.620
All we need to do is
bolt the docker image

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:14.700
that produces this.

00:45:14.700 --> 00:45:16.710
So in this case, the
end user can quickly

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:22.740
analyze the model itself
and just use this existing

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:25.770
static HTML and data set.

00:45:25.770 --> 00:45:27.630
And then a more powerful
thing that Aanand

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:29.400
was showcasing
earlier is the fact

00:45:29.400 --> 00:45:32.053
that we have tensor
boards available to us.

00:45:32.053 --> 00:45:33.720
So at the training
step, we can actually

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:35.430
click on Open TensorBoard.

00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:37.640
It's one of the artifacts
that's produced,

00:45:37.640 --> 00:45:41.780
and the end user can then draw
into this and, in more detail,

00:45:41.780 --> 00:45:45.150
analyze their model and the
specific TensorFlow run,

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:48.730
the distributions, histograms,
and anything like that.

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:54.180
So I'd implore you to have a
look at Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:46:00.300
And in fact, if we go
back to our presentation,

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:03.180
you can easily deploy a
Kubeflow Pipelines using

00:46:03.180 --> 00:46:05.130
the deploy.kubeflow.cloud.

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:05.980
It's very simple.

00:46:05.980 --> 00:46:08.280
It'll only take you one
or two minutes to do that.

00:46:08.280 --> 00:46:13.260
And yeah, I encourage
you to give it a go.

00:46:13.260 --> 00:46:14.160
Thanks.

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:16.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:17.160 --> 00:46:20.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

